1
00:01:30,640 --> 00:01:36,980
Path of Blood

2
00:01:58,330 --> 00:02:01,130
Are you deaf too, you old man?

3
00:02:09,870 --> 00:02:11,640
Don't move, guards.

4
00:02:14,910 --> 00:02:18,010
The king is within reach of my sword.

5
00:02:22,340 --> 00:02:23,470
Is he serious?

6
00:02:23,470 --> 00:02:24,660
Oh, dear.

7
00:02:37,080 --> 00:02:38,250
Askeladd.

8
00:02:39,580 --> 00:02:41,700
You bastard! King Sweyn is mine.

9
00:02:41,700 --> 00:02:42,920
Don't move, Thorkell!

10
00:02:42,920 --> 00:02:44,860
Askeladd's not acting normal.

11
00:02:44,860 --> 00:02:46,300
Don't provoke him.

12
00:02:46,300 --> 00:02:48,550
The king mustn't be harmed.

13
00:02:48,930 --> 00:02:49,910
What?

14
00:02:56,590 --> 00:03:00,570
Put away your sword,
then I'll forgive you for this offense.

15
00:03:01,190 --> 00:03:02,820
Forgive me?

16
00:03:02,820 --> 00:03:05,110
Don't push your luck, you savage.

17
00:03:05,700 --> 00:03:10,620
As punishment for mocking my family
and my people, you deserve to die!

18
00:03:11,300 --> 00:03:12,910
This is my last warning.

19
00:03:13,710 --> 00:03:17,120
Put away your sword, Askeladd.

20
00:03:17,500 --> 00:03:19,450
That's my nickname.

21
00:03:20,120 --> 00:03:23,880
I'll tell you the name my mother gave me.
My real name.

22
00:03:25,640 --> 00:03:28,630
Lucius Artorius Castus.

23
00:03:29,390 --> 00:03:35,480
I am the rightful king of the land of Britannia.

24
00:03:52,910 --> 00:03:54,600
I feel better now.

25
00:04:18,330 --> 00:04:23,690
For a period of time,
the Danes held dominion over England.

26
00:04:24,130 --> 00:04:28,590
But in the year 1014,
the situation changed rapidly

27
00:04:28,590 --> 00:04:31,460
with the death of King Sweyn of Denmark.

28
00:04:36,390 --> 00:04:41,730
Denmark and England
crossed swords once again.

29
00:04:43,030 --> 00:04:45,500
Canute succeeded his father Sweyn

30
00:04:45,500 --> 00:04:50,060
and took command of the
Danish viking army in England.

31
00:04:51,200 --> 00:04:53,010
Upon hearing news of Sweyn's death,

32
00:04:53,010 --> 00:04:58,550
Ethelred II returned from his exile
to Normandy and was reinstated.

33
00:04:59,640 --> 00:05:03,790
Both men proclaimed themselves
King of England,

34
00:05:03,790 --> 00:05:08,010
and the fate of the throne
was uncertain once more.

35
00:05:08,560 --> 00:05:13,750
England was exhausted
by never-ending battles.

36
00:05:22,190 --> 00:05:23,980
Your Highness, stand back.

37
00:05:23,980 --> 00:05:27,130
I'm fine. I didn't drink any.

38
00:05:27,130 --> 00:05:29,380
Y-Your Highness, have mercy!

39
00:05:29,740 --> 00:05:33,680
I swear to our Lord Jesus Christ
that I didn't know.

40
00:05:33,680 --> 00:05:35,680
I had no idea the drink was poisoned.

41
00:05:36,110 --> 00:05:37,970
Your Highness, have mercy!

42
00:05:37,970 --> 00:05:39,770
Please, King Ethelred!

43
00:05:40,590 --> 00:05:42,110
Bastard...

44
00:05:43,710 --> 00:05:45,480
Such underhanded means...

45
00:06:11,230 --> 00:06:17,950
The situation here is even worse
than we were told, Your Highness.

46
00:06:18,910 --> 00:06:21,850
It will take a long time for Mercia,

47
00:06:22,210 --> 00:06:27,290
once called the Jewel of the Fields,
to recover from this.

48
00:06:28,710 --> 00:06:31,580
Gunnar, don't speak as though
we had nothing to do with this.

49
00:06:32,220 --> 00:06:34,030
We laid waste to this region.

50
00:06:36,830 --> 00:06:37,930
What is that?

51
00:06:38,720 --> 00:06:39,550
Huh?

52
00:06:40,570 --> 00:06:42,250
Oh, right.

53
00:06:42,620 --> 00:06:47,500
This is the advance force of our army,
which conquered this region.

54
00:06:47,950 --> 00:06:49,420
That's not what I meant.

55
00:06:49,420 --> 00:06:51,760
I'm asking what it is that they're doing.

56
00:06:52,450 --> 00:06:55,220
Why, pillaging the land, of course.

57
00:06:55,220 --> 00:06:58,790
They're looking for girls to
sell from among the beggars.

58
00:06:59,690 --> 00:07:01,110
I thought I forbade that.

59
00:07:01,950 --> 00:07:04,600
Have them beheaded and
display their heads on the road.

60
00:07:04,600 --> 00:07:05,560
What?

61
00:07:06,040 --> 00:07:11,200
B-But collecting the spoils
of war is a military custom.

62
00:07:11,200 --> 00:07:13,730
Cracking down on it harshly would be...

63
00:07:13,730 --> 00:07:16,550
Men who can't maintain discipline
are no soldiers of mine.

64
00:07:17,020 --> 00:07:19,580
Behead them and display
their heads on the road.

65
00:07:20,220 --> 00:07:23,710
Y-Yes, Your Highness.

66
00:07:23,710 --> 00:07:24,650
Hey, you!

67
00:07:24,650 --> 00:07:26,870
What? Seriously?

68
00:07:42,430 --> 00:07:46,730
I appreciate you coming all the
way here, Your Highness.

69
00:07:47,370 --> 00:07:49,860
Floki, have the arrangements been made?

70
00:07:50,210 --> 00:07:52,850
Yes, Your Highness, they await your command.

71
00:07:52,850 --> 00:07:55,740
They can move out immediately.

72
00:07:56,760 --> 00:08:00,540
But you need not have met them yourself.

73
00:08:00,540 --> 00:08:02,620
You could have allowed us to handle the matter.

74
00:08:02,620 --> 00:08:06,110
It's fine. I was already here
observing the military situation.

75
00:08:06,110 --> 00:08:07,250
I shall meet with them.

76
00:08:07,580 --> 00:08:10,530
Earl Eadric of Mercia is a coward.

77
00:08:10,530 --> 00:08:12,870
He is hardly a man worth meeting.

78
00:08:13,440 --> 00:08:16,860
A person's character and their
usefulness are two different things.

79
00:08:16,860 --> 00:08:19,130
Either way, I shall meet him
and judge for myself.

80
00:08:21,100 --> 00:08:26,190
And besides, there is
another reason for my visit.

81
00:08:27,330 --> 00:08:29,320
What do you mean?

82
00:08:30,340 --> 00:08:33,270
Hey!

83
00:08:33,270 --> 00:08:37,280
Canute!

84
00:08:37,280 --> 00:08:40,530
You're here, you bastard!

85
00:08:40,530 --> 00:08:44,280
Canute!

86
00:08:44,950 --> 00:08:47,220
I must appease that.

87
00:08:47,220 --> 00:08:49,680
I imagine it was too great a
task for you and your men.

88
00:08:49,680 --> 00:08:52,080
Yes, indeed.

89
00:09:02,590 --> 00:09:05,470
You little brat!

90
00:09:05,470 --> 00:09:06,910
Let me hit him!

91
00:09:06,910 --> 00:09:11,400
Get outta my way and let me hit him once!

92
00:09:12,060 --> 00:09:13,480
Calm yourself, Thorkell.

93
00:09:14,020 --> 00:09:17,090
Why, you friggin' fraggin' little...

94
00:09:18,090 --> 00:09:25,740
I hate when someone stops my
battles more than I hate frogs!

95
00:09:26,080 --> 00:09:26,910
What?

96
00:09:26,910 --> 00:09:27,990
Frogs?

97
00:09:29,130 --> 00:09:31,950
Mercia has requested a truce.

98
00:09:32,860 --> 00:09:35,630
Our victory here is as good as certain.

99
00:09:36,030 --> 00:09:38,010
You did well, Thorkell.

100
00:09:38,010 --> 00:09:40,010
Shut up!

101
00:09:40,750 --> 00:09:47,100
Battles don't stop until you've
taken the opposing leader's head.

102
00:09:47,100 --> 00:09:51,770
I was about to lop off Eadric's head.

103
00:09:52,180 --> 00:09:53,730
Don't make such a fuss.

104
00:09:53,730 --> 00:09:57,320
Earl Eadric of Mercia is
merely the leader of this region.

105
00:09:57,760 --> 00:09:59,480
Look at the bigger picture.

106
00:10:00,030 --> 00:10:03,600
I don't care about that crap.

107
00:10:03,600 --> 00:10:07,530
I just want to enjoy a
fulfilling life of warfare.

108
00:10:07,900 --> 00:10:13,420
In the lands to the east, Ethelred's son,
Edmund, is making some disquieting moves.

109
00:10:13,870 --> 00:10:18,840
I ask that you help us pacify the east.
Your overwhelming strength in battle is—

110
00:10:18,840 --> 00:10:24,380
I'll decide where I go and
who I fight, Dicehead.

111
00:10:25,500 --> 00:10:26,800
Canute!

112
00:10:27,410 --> 00:10:32,560
You'd better not have forgotten
what I said to you then.

113
00:10:33,670 --> 00:10:40,950
"I'm going to watch what you do from now on,
and who you'll become."

114
00:10:42,440 --> 00:10:45,260
You're still a nobody.

115
00:10:45,260 --> 00:10:47,820
You aren't even the King of England.

116
00:10:48,140 --> 00:10:50,490
You're just a kid.

117
00:10:50,850 --> 00:10:52,880
So listen up, kid.

118
00:10:52,880 --> 00:10:56,730
No matter what you do,
you won't be able to avoid war.

119
00:10:57,170 --> 00:11:00,710
You have no choice but to carve
your path with blood and iron.

120
00:11:01,640 --> 00:11:04,100
Just like Askeladd did.

121
00:11:04,100 --> 00:11:05,630
Don't forget that.

122
00:11:06,080 --> 00:11:10,360
From the start, the path you walk
has been covered in blood.

123
00:11:12,660 --> 00:11:14,350
How long are you going to keep doing that?

124
00:11:14,350 --> 00:11:16,850
It's annoying! Get outta here!

125
00:11:17,200 --> 00:11:19,350
Boy, I'm pissed.

126
00:11:20,020 --> 00:11:23,990
I'll back down for today,
but there won't be a second time.

127
00:11:23,990 --> 00:11:25,640
And don't you forget it.

128
00:11:27,490 --> 00:11:30,580
H-How dare he insult you like that
in front of the soldiers.

129
00:11:30,580 --> 00:11:35,500
Your Highness,
you mustn't overlook this offense.

130
00:11:36,230 --> 00:11:38,930
Why not? It makes sense.

131
00:11:38,930 --> 00:11:40,750
He offered a valuable warning.

132
00:11:41,820 --> 00:11:43,830
He's an honest man.

133
00:11:42,440 --> 00:11:43,830
Move it!

134
00:11:44,540 --> 00:11:45,980
Clear the way.

135
00:11:44,540 --> 00:11:47,380
It would surely be a
pleasure to live like him.

136
00:11:50,830 --> 00:11:51,770
Let's go.

137
00:12:15,080 --> 00:12:17,430
4,000 pounds of silver.

138
00:12:17,870 --> 00:12:21,190
This is everything we were
able to gather at this time.

139
00:12:22,200 --> 00:12:24,250
Please accept this offering.

140
00:12:28,810 --> 00:12:32,760
If you'll withdraw your forces from Mercia,

141
00:12:33,090 --> 00:12:35,740
we'll provide you with an
additional 4,000 pounds.

142
00:12:35,740 --> 00:12:39,070
It's not a bad deal, Canute.

143
00:12:40,090 --> 00:12:44,510
You offer me Danegeld, Eadric?

144
00:12:48,540 --> 00:12:52,140
What is your answer, Canute?

145
00:13:07,450 --> 00:13:08,920
This is nothing.

146
00:13:22,070 --> 00:13:25,440
Okay, lift 'em.

147
00:13:36,620 --> 00:13:37,400
Hup.

148
00:14:13,160 --> 00:14:14,770
Let me ask you something.

149
00:14:16,170 --> 00:14:24,250
You're paying me this silver so
I'll leave Mercia, aren't you, Eadric?

150
00:14:24,830 --> 00:14:27,250
I-I'm offering you 8,000 pounds.

151
00:14:27,250 --> 00:14:30,340
That should be more than enough
for you to withdraw.

152
00:14:32,570 --> 00:14:33,470
It's not.

153
00:14:34,750 --> 00:14:37,880
Think carefully about what you're saying.

154
00:14:38,370 --> 00:14:42,480
You're telling Canute, Bretwalda of England,

155
00:14:42,830 --> 00:14:46,470
to take this money
and leave his own territory.

156
00:14:47,290 --> 00:14:51,740
And the price you set on such an
insult against a king is this paltry sum?

157
00:14:55,060 --> 00:14:57,350
I am your king.

158
00:14:58,190 --> 00:15:02,520
I came to this land to quell the rebellion
taking place within my domain,

159
00:15:03,750 --> 00:15:05,710
not for wealth.

160
00:15:09,080 --> 00:15:10,380
This is nonsense!

161
00:15:10,380 --> 00:15:16,510
Our king is Ethelred of the
honorable House of Wessex, not you.

162
00:15:16,510 --> 00:15:17,920
Silence.

163
00:15:17,920 --> 00:15:19,380
But Father...

164
00:15:19,380 --> 00:15:22,580
Enough. This is neither the time nor place.

165
00:15:22,580 --> 00:15:27,770
You're right. There is another
who calls himself the King of England.

166
00:15:29,510 --> 00:15:34,650
So, did you offer me this deal
with Ethelred's permission?

167
00:15:36,270 --> 00:15:41,280
I-I'm sure His Highness would understand
if we explained the situation to him.

168
00:15:42,330 --> 00:15:45,290
Ah, how open-minded of him.

169
00:15:45,930 --> 00:15:51,470
So Ethelred would forgive one
of his own vassals for deserting

170
00:15:51,470 --> 00:15:53,580
in order to protect his own land.

171
00:15:54,920 --> 00:15:58,220
Now, that's not nice.

172
00:15:58,220 --> 00:15:59,870
He's got a nasty streak.

173
00:16:02,310 --> 00:16:05,230
The world is in chaos
because there are two kings.

174
00:16:07,700 --> 00:16:10,920
One must die.

175
00:16:12,840 --> 00:16:16,940
Ethelred is a coward who
abandoned his people and throne

176
00:16:16,940 --> 00:16:20,150
to flee into exile during the previous war.

177
00:16:21,060 --> 00:16:26,970
Don't you think it's foolish to trust
the future of England to such a man?

178
00:16:29,390 --> 00:16:33,590
You're telling me to betray him?

179
00:16:37,310 --> 00:16:39,470
You've already betrayed him.

180
00:16:42,380 --> 00:16:45,820
I'd like you to assist me, Eadric.

181
00:16:50,670 --> 00:16:54,040
I'd like to make Ethelred go away.

182
00:16:57,190 --> 00:17:01,010
You've served him for
many years as his vassal.

183
00:17:01,010 --> 00:17:02,990
I'm sure you would know
of a good method.

184
00:17:05,960 --> 00:17:11,750
Do you intend to strip me of
every last shred of loyalty, boy?

185
00:17:12,890 --> 00:17:15,350
In exchange, I'll allow you to keep Mercia.

186
00:17:15,900 --> 00:17:18,040
Ethelred's head is my price.

187
00:17:18,760 --> 00:17:20,760
A good deal, don't you think?

188
00:17:21,870 --> 00:17:26,190
I refuse. A truce is off the table.

189
00:17:26,190 --> 00:17:30,270
I'll keep fighting a foul man
like you until the bitter end.

190
00:17:35,850 --> 00:17:40,110
Floki, this man doesn't seem to be a coward.

191
00:17:40,110 --> 00:17:41,610
He's got some backbone.

192
00:17:42,580 --> 00:17:47,880
In that case,
shall we test it, Your Highness?

193
00:17:51,660 --> 00:17:53,540
Come outside, Eadric.

194
00:17:54,250 --> 00:17:56,360
I have something to show you.

195
00:18:42,570 --> 00:18:44,050
Are you ready?

196
00:18:44,050 --> 00:18:45,590
Yes, Your Highness.

197
00:18:47,610 --> 00:18:48,470
Light it.

198
00:19:02,520 --> 00:19:06,490
Signal fires? What are they signaling?

199
00:19:09,630 --> 00:19:12,730
What is this?

200
00:19:15,420 --> 00:19:19,110
What are you doing, Canute?

201
00:19:33,390 --> 00:19:34,550
Father.

202
00:19:36,630 --> 00:19:38,940
L-Look...

203
00:19:51,320 --> 00:19:53,310
Th-There are more over there.

204
00:19:57,630 --> 00:20:02,890
C-Canute, what are they signaling?

205
00:20:06,840 --> 00:20:09,770
This is what it means
to go to war against me.

206
00:20:11,910 --> 00:20:14,410
Imagine it, Eadric.

207
00:20:15,800 --> 00:20:20,840
Mercia has been reduced to ash.

208
00:20:36,330 --> 00:20:37,580
Do you understand now?

209
00:20:38,410 --> 00:20:44,340
If I will it, reducing you to ash
would be no trouble at all.

210
00:20:51,510 --> 00:20:52,670
Father!

211
00:20:59,740 --> 00:21:03,230
Buy peace by offering me Ethelred's head.

212
00:21:04,030 --> 00:21:05,550
It's the smart choice.

213
00:21:32,810 --> 00:21:41,310
The next year, in April of 1016,
King Ethelred II of England would die.

214
00:21:49,320 --> 00:21:50,300
Your Highness!

215
00:21:52,520 --> 00:21:54,460
It is said he died of an illness.

216
00:22:03,580 --> 00:22:08,920
After Ethelred's death,
his son Edmund assumed the throne.

217
00:22:09,380 --> 00:22:12,350
As his father's successor,

218
00:22:12,350 --> 00:22:15,930
Edmund caused great trouble for
Canute's forces in a hard-fought war, but...

219
00:22:21,170 --> 00:22:27,190
Seven months later in that same year,
he unsurprisingly also died of an illness.

220
00:22:40,280 --> 00:22:42,110
Died of an illness, huh?

221
00:22:42,810 --> 00:22:46,080
It took King Sweyn ten years
to conquer the nation,

222
00:22:46,590 --> 00:22:48,840
and this time, it ended in less than three.

223
00:22:50,090 --> 00:22:55,310
The Witanagemot will recognize
Canute as Bretwalda.

224
00:22:56,060 --> 00:22:58,270
The coronation will be held
first thing next year.

225
00:23:03,770 --> 00:23:05,750
Don't talk to me about Canute.

226
00:23:06,270 --> 00:23:07,710
You're ruining my beer.

227
00:23:12,360 --> 00:23:13,490
Fine, fine.

228
00:23:28,840 --> 00:23:34,550
Sweyn, Ethelred, and Edmund...

229
00:23:36,090 --> 00:23:39,070
Thanks to the unnatural deaths of his rivals,

230
00:23:39,070 --> 00:23:45,350
in the year 1018, Canute officially
became King of England.

231
00:25:36,170 --> 00:25:39,970
We Need a Horse

232
00:25:37,920 --> 00:25:39,320
"We Need a Horse."
